ith the new loss function or or  live with with our previous one and um  uh popular libraries uh come with uh  dozens of different loss functions kind  of baked in because people want the  flexibility around these things  so um in this work we're trying to  present a new paradigm  for learning that will overcome this  obstacle and we define this object that  we call an omni predictor so an  omnipredictor is a  is a  predictor that um  the idea is that we learn it and then  given a loss function  we can have a  a simple univariate transformation over  the output of this predictor that  depends on the loss function but does  not depend on the data itself  and it would be  competitive with the best  predictor  from that family c  for that loss function l  so in other words what the omnipredictor  does is it extracts all the predictive  power of c for that family of loss  functions l  and then we can learn the predictor once  and uh we compute the actual hypothesis  when the loss function is determined so  once we determine what the loss function  is  there should be a simple way to uh  transform the omni predictor to a  predictor for that specific loss  function  um  now the first thought is why would  something like this even exist  and if you think about it for a minute  then uh um then something like this does  exist and it's actually um maybe not  phrased as an omni predictor but it's  kind of known  uh um as folk or or  or is used in practice and that is the  ground truth predictor so  let's say that we  can really compute the probability of  the label being one given a feature  a feature set x  so if we had that p star x that ground  truth if we were really able to compute  it  then  you can observe that given a point x the  expected contribution of that point x to  the loss function  is actually dependent only on the loss  function  so  if we could have computed uh this uh  ground truth predictor then in fact we  would have had  an omni predictor for essentially for  all loss functions and for all um and  for all  model families  um  and  the main problem here is that the ground  truth predictor typically just cannot be  computed efficiently unless there are  really uh very benign circumstances or  very special uh  and strong assumptions there is no  reason to think that we can per point  per point x compute the ground truth the  real probability that um uh that the  label is one  it does present a very tempting  heuristic to use so we have this family  seed let's say you know deep neural nets  or whatever um and we can compute uh  the best hopefully we can compute the  best predictor from this family  for uh the l2 loss and the reason i  chose the l2 loss is that it is actually  minimized by the actual ground truth so  it is kind of an approximation of the  ground truth with respect to this family  c or so we hope and we can use any  proper loss function so any loss  function that is optimized by the ground  truth's predictions  and now let's use c star  as if it was the ground truth and output  and hypothesis and just hope that it's  good so  so we hope that now this hypothesis  would would be a an omni predictor for  the set see  the problem is that this uh heuristic  even though it's very tempting and it's  actually being used in practice in  various places  does not carry any guarantee and  there are examples that i don't have  time to go through where we can show  that this  heuristic fails  so  in some sense our contribution is to  find out  what predictor actually does work for  this family c um and how to how to  compute it  and in order to describe that i have to  make a very very brief side tour  so there is another property of  predictors that comes from a completely  different  line of thinking that of  of fairness  and that is the properties of  calibration and multi-calibration  so  a predictor is calibrated if it is  correct on expectation  in some sense so let's say we want to  predict the weather for tomorrow so  and we predict in many different cities  and many places and what we want is  let's look at all the places or all the  days where we predicted that rain will  fall 10 with probability 10 percent we  want 10 of these  uh of these days to to be correct for  the rain to actually fall and that  should be true for every prediction that  we give  so a prediction a predictor that has  this property is called calibrated  um and it's clearly a property that we  would like  the thing is that on its own it's very  weak so we want to require something  more from the from a predictor  in particular just giving the  expectation of the population is a  calibrated predictor which is very  benign and very weak from an accuracy  point of view  but this definition could be extended to  the notion of multi-calibration so now  we think of our model family not as just  a set of functions but as a set of  subsets so it's binary functions for now  and and we say that a predictor t is  multi-calibrated with respect to c if it  is calibrated not just for the general  population but for every set from c  separately when uh when we take our  distribution and conditioned it on on  getting samples from that particular set  and the definition could be extended for  c that are real functions um  but it's useful to think uh now as just  a calibration over subsets  and um our theorem  is that  these uh  these multi-calibrated uh predictors are  the right predictors for uh our loss  minimization goal so in fact a  multi-calibrated predictor is an lc omni  predictor so the c is the same c the  same set of models for which we computed  the multi-calibration predictor  and the l the family of loss functions  for which we are an omni predictor is  all convex loss functions um and and we  show that convexity is indeed required  here  and i'd like to point that there are  known algorithms to compute a  multi-calibrated predictor under some  standard assumptions but it is not a  trivial task it it 